      <!-- Section 3: The Secret Passport Stamp Rule -->
      <h2>3. The Secret On-Board Border Control Rule (Crucial Tip!)</h2>

      <p>Nearly every first-time traveler expects Moroccan border police to check passports in a booth at the arrival terminal after disembarking. <strong>This is NOT how the Tarifa-to-Tangier ferry operates!</strong></p>

      <div class="roam-callout-ai">
        <strong>⚠️ Mandatory On-Board Passport Stamping:</strong>
        <br>On the Tarifa &rarr; Tangier Ville ferry, <strong>Moroccan passport control takes place inside the vessel while the ship is sailing across the strait</strong>.
        <ol style="margin-top:10px;margin-bottom:0;">
          <li>As soon as you board the catamaran in Tarifa, pick up a white <em>Fiche de Débarquement</em> (entry slip) from the counter and fill out your details (name, passport number, hotel address in Morocco).</li>
          <li>Locate the Moroccan police booth situated inside the main passenger cabin (usually near the duty-free shop or cafeteria).</li>
          <li>Stand in line immediately. The officer inspects your passport, verifies your entry card, and stamps your passport with your unique Moroccan national entry number (N° d'Immatriculation).</li>
          <li><strong>If you do not get this stamp before the ferry docks</strong>, you will not be allowed to exit the port gate in Tangier and will be sent back onto the ship or held until the next sailing's police officer arrives!</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <!-- Section 4: Car Rentals vs Private Driver -->
      <h2>4. Can You Bring a European Rental Car into Morocco?</h2>

      <p>A frequent inquiry from self-drive road-trippers exploring Andalusia is: <em>"Can I drive my Spanish rental car onto the ferry to explore Chefchaouen and Fes?"</em></p>

      <p>The short answer is: <strong>Almost never.</strong></p>

      <p>Major rental car agencies in Spain (Hertz, Avis, Europcar, Sixt, Enterprise) strictly forbid taking their vehicles outside the European Union. Their insurance coverage terminates the moment the car rolls onto the ferry. Furthermore, taking a car through Moroccan customs at Tanger Med requires temporary import permits (D16 ter form) that require the driver to be the legal owner listed on the vehicle registration (<em>Permiso de Circulación</em>).</p>

      <p><strong>The Sensible Solution:</strong> Park your Spanish rental car in the secure multi-story parking garage at the Port of Tarifa (approx. €16–€20/day) or drop it off in Seville/Málaga and take a comfortable bus to Tarifa. Then, book a dedicated <a href="/morocco-private-driver.html">private driver with an air-conditioned luxury vehicle</a> to meet you at the port in Tangier.</p>

